#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198075 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198433 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5329867-1956-5f36-8614-26d483c36efe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/id #uuid "89942534-6b03-52ec-8494-830de5eb8128" :question/math-academy-id "e-12558" :question/problem "Find the points of the intersection of the elliptic cone $x^2 + y^2=z^2$ and the line $x +1=y-1 =\\dfrac{z}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n![](images/b0/b0a31426fa8f1d55dcc4a94fb7b6544cdba753d879a4b95c67b66662ce43a26e.svg)\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}+(t+1)^{2} & =(2t)^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}-2t+1+{t}^{2}+2t+1 & =4{t}^{2} \\\\ 2{t}^{2}+2 & =4{t}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =2{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =1 \\\\ t & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-1-1=-2 \\\\ y=-1+1=0 \\\\ z=2(-1)=-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $\\left(-2,0,-2\\right).$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=1-1=0 \\\\ y=1+1=2 \\\\ z=2(1)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $\\left(0,2,2\\right).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,0,-2)$ and $(0,2,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198228 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198349 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a426dc8c-2be0-5e83-93b6-54a634561aad" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/id #uuid "5524aeab-cc64-5da8-bea3-8cd5dd86ebcb" :question/math-academy-id "e-5264" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\dfrac{x^2}{24} + \\dfrac{y^2}{24} +\\dfrac{z^2}{6} = 1$ and the line \n$\\{\\begin{aligned}x=4t \\\\ y=8t, \\\\ z=2t\\end{aligned}$ \nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{24}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{24}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =24 \\\\ (4t)^{2}+(8t)^{2}+4(2t)^{2} & =24 \\\\ 16{t}^{2}+64{t}^{2}+16{t}^{2} & =24 \\\\ 96{t}^{2} & =24 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solution $t = \\pm \\dfrac{1}{2}.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\dfrac 1 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=4(-\\frac{1}{2})=-2 \\\\ y=8(-\\frac{1}{2})=-4 \\\\ z=2(-\\frac{1}{2})=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $\\left(-2,-4,-1\\right).$\n\n- For $t=\\dfrac 1 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=4(\\frac{1}{2})=2 \\\\ y=8(\\frac{1}{2})=4 \\\\ z=2(\\frac{1}{2})=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $\\left(2,4,1\\right).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,-4,-1)$ and $(2,4,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198229 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198354 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58427e6a-8e77-5c01-a578-a7dc4b8f8812" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Projection of the Intersection of a Surface and a Tilted Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "b1f59935-cd61-5c59-aa8d-cf7ba7f151bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-5618" :question/problem "Find the projection of the intersection between the ellipsoid $\\dfrac{(x+1)^2}{16}+\\dfrac{y^2}{4}+\\dfrac{z^2}{16}=1$ and the plane $x-z=3$ onto the $yz$-plane." :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-z=3\\,⟹\\,x=z+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{((z+3)+1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(z+4)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}^{2}+8z+16}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{2{z}^{2}+8z+16}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}+4z+8}{8} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}+4z+8}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{({z}^{2}+4z+4)+4}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(z+2)^{2}+4}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(z+2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{1}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(z+2)^{2}}{8} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(z+2)^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\dfrac{y^2}{2}+\\dfrac{(z+2)^2}{4} = 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496282 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198354 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58427e6a-8e77-5c01-a578-a7dc4b8f8812" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Projection of the Intersection of a Surface and a Tilted Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2903bd31-59f2-5edf-ba78-0b700636216f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495599} {:answer/id #uuid "72fcd5f6-74c1-548c-b798-b9f86c8b5a52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495763} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e331d6-2b84-5961-a705-7c0440233497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495990} {:answer/id #uuid "e4145407-31af-5f0e-be8b-a08fc7a69e9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496016} {:answer/id #uuid "e87b73a0-162f-5a86-8eb0-04ffa6648475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{8}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9e331d6-2b84-5961-a705-7c0440233497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495990} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac07d115-2c5e-5c77-b205-49ff29ccdb65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c16a549-c615-5f5a-9201-b01b9bbc4908" :question/math-academy-id "q-36325" :question/problem "Find the projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of one sheet \n$(x+3)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-2{z}^{2}=1$\n and the plane   $x+2z=-3$ onto the\n$xy$-plane." :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $z$, as follows:\n\n$$\nx+2z=-3\\,⟹\\,z=-\\frac{x+3}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the hyperboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-2{(-\\frac{x+3}{2})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{2}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496264 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198349 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a426dc8c-2be0-5e83-93b6-54a634561aad" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80350002-a1a3-5d74-bd16-1ec73fee04f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ and $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495786} {:answer/id #uuid "8f8e83f7-8310-58f5-9c73-52c7cc216ae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ and $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495826} {:answer/id #uuid "9823e226-22f2-5864-b4b5-cc7c8eaef60c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495852} {:answer/id #uuid "9d651deb-dd53-5f49-8875-9a85b310492f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ and $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495860} {:answer/id #uuid "ce78662c-56d5-5bd3-8e1e-31dfb2b4b788" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d651deb-dd53-5f49-8875-9a85b310492f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ and $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495860} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1dd5380-2900-5587-97bf-5ddd6b25c63f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6022ca77-a6e7-5c06-a55c-446e2e02d3c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-71123" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}+3{z}^{2}=1$ and the line \n$\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-t \\\\ y=t, \\\\ z=t\\end{aligned}$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}+3{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+6{z}^{2} & =2 \\\\ (-t)^{2}+2(t)^{2}+6(t)^{2} & =2 \\\\ {t}^{2}+2{t}^{2}+6{t}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 9{t}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ and $t=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ z=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}).$\n\n- For $t=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ z=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ and $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198349 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a426dc8c-2be0-5e83-93b6-54a634561aad" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19504466-0a10-574f-bb8c-3c8ded8f176c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,1,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483609} {:answer/id #uuid "3402b0ae-2770-548b-bd7e-08caf8efcf00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,1,0)$ and $(-2,3,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483661} {:answer/id #uuid "476cbccb-7f02-581b-b415-12ad49fd0514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2,-1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483697} {:answer/id #uuid "85f544ac-b2af-53d2-9846-ab93a0ca63da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,-1,1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483839} {:answer/id #uuid "85fe0a6f-92b1-5ed1-85c4-d3ebde706c06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,3,-2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19504466-0a10-574f-bb8c-3c8ded8f176c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,1,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483609} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f0fad20-662a-5ac9-aa37-fdaf4bd1c44b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bf7120f6-4fae-5d19-b842-ba88886669a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-71129" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1$ and the line \n$\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2t \\\\ y=2-t, \\\\ z=t-1\\end{aligned}$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2} & =8 \\\\ (2t)^{2}+4(2-t)^{2}+2(t-1)^{2} & =8 \\\\ 4{t}^{2}+4(4-4t+{t}^{2})+2({t}^{2}-2t+1) & =8 \\\\ 4{t}^{2}+16-16t+4{t}^{2}+2{t}^{2}-4t+2 & =8 \\\\ 10{t}^{2}-20t+18 & =8 \\\\ 10{t}^{2}-20t+10 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}-2t+1 & =0 \\\\ (t-1)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solution $t=1.$\n\nNow, we substitute our solution back into the equations of the line.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2(1)=2 \\\\ y=2-1=1 \\\\ z=1-1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(2,1,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484215 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198354 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58427e6a-8e77-5c01-a578-a7dc4b8f8812" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Projection of the Intersection of a Surface and a Tilted Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a93ae61-117e-52eb-a561-05bda1830dfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,4)$ in the $yz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483674} {:answer/id #uuid "3fa19c57-773f-538a-8210-ed0794e91c6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,4)$ in the $yz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483688} {:answer/id #uuid "5a3c9888-3e12-5f64-b8ce-fec1d37e0fa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-4)$ in the $yz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483736} {:answer/id #uuid "8eb0748f-88f4-5a20-a136-b18b07e72b94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-4)$ in the $yz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483864} {:answer/id #uuid "a1b4f9f0-5d69-54f2-a20f-cb95071368a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,1)$ in the $yz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8eb0748f-88f4-5a20-a136-b18b07e72b94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-4)$ in the $yz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483864} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf6552b0-6071-5cb6-8a4f-6d1b9d70cb89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3f2db1db-7bfe-5b3b-ab6d-846d64109b8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-72342" :question/problem "For the elliptic paraboloid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{5}=z+4$, the section in the plane $\\sqrt{5}x+4y=4$ is a parabola. \nFind the vertex of the projection of this parabola onto $yz$-plane." :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}x+4y=4\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{4-4y}{\\sqrt{5}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{(\\frac{4-4y}{\\sqrt{5}})}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{5} & =z+4 \\\\ \\frac{4(1-y)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{5} & =z+4 \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =z+4 \\\\ z & =(y-1)^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the vertex of the projection onto $yz$-plane is $(1,-4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375570 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198433 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5329867-1956-5f36-8614-26d483c36efe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c1a8864-0937-5a93-b9ed-6eb831acf7c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(12,1,1)$ and $(4,-1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375572} {:answer/id #uuid "2a8ad80b-85a9-584c-8568-2b5e5a98f98a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-2,-2)$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375573} {:answer/id #uuid "02e1c7c6-dccd-53e2-9aec-f9596ed92975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,-3,-3)$   only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375574} {:answer/id #uuid "6bad198b-b214-5b1f-b47a-16e32392d56f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(16,2,2)$  and $(0,-2,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375575} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd33eb2-e44d-593e-9dc2-78bb8af6fff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(4,-1,-1)$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a8ad80b-85a9-584c-8568-2b5e5a98f98a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-2,-2)$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375573} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dd145b8-554f-5187-843c-4effb83714b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9105e6ec-384a-5ac8-9a8d-8fcef2e66571" :question/math-academy-id "q-94786" :question/problem "Find the point of the intersection of the elliptic cone ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=3{z}^{2}$ and the line $\\frac{x-8}{4}=y=z.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-8}{4}=y=z=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=4t+8 \\\\ y=t \\\\ z=t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =3{z}^{2} \\\\ (4t+8)^{2}+3{t}^{2} & =3{t}^{2} \\\\ (4t+8)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 4t+8 & =0 \\\\ 4t & =-8 \\\\ t & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line. For $t=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=4(-2)+8=0 \\\\ y=-2 \\\\ z=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(0,-2,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496297 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198433 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5329867-1956-5f36-8614-26d483c36efe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "546e85b6-9a17-5aec-bbd9-992ec66bc6e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-12,0,0)$  and $(0,16,16)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495701} {:answer/id #uuid "556606cd-8bf1-52d3-a524-8dd1d0277946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,16,16)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495703} {:answer/id #uuid "63edc0ae-558d-55a3-8754-742bb098f1f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-6,8,8)$  and $(-12,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495732} {:answer/id #uuid "84bbd939-9c9c-56e5-b704-1f1882c462eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-6,8,8)$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495796} {:answer/id #uuid "a231fce3-6386-5514-9985-ec37dfb37620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-9,4,4)$    only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "556606cd-8bf1-52d3-a524-8dd1d0277946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,16,16)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495703} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e4ffe0a-6381-57a7-ad0d-2a73b14d4252" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac5f0783-86c6-5b1d-9dbf-c5b648b83eaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-94789" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the elliptic cone $3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=2{z}^{2}$ and the line $\\frac{x+12}{3}=\\frac{y}{4}=\\frac{z}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+12}{3}=\\frac{y}{4}=\\frac{z}{4}=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=3t-12 \\\\ y=4t \\\\ z=4t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & =2{z}^{2} \\\\ 3(3t-12)^{2}+2(4t)^{2} & =2(4t)^{2} \\\\ 3(3t-12)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (3t-12)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3t-12 & =0 \\\\ 3t & =12 \\\\ t & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solution back into the equations of the line. For $t=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=3(4)-12=0 \\\\ y=4(4)=16 \\\\ z=4(4)=16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(0,16,16).$"}]}
